We are tasked with finding the largest subset of the integers from \(1\) to \(16\) such that no two integers in this subset have a product that is a perfect square.

### Step 2: Mathematical Concepts and Theorems
A product of two integers \(a\) and \(b\) is a perfect square if and only if all the prime factors in their prime factorization have even exponents when combined. This means that for any two integers \(a\) and \(b\), if we consider their prime factorization:
\[
a = p_1^{e_1} p_2^{e_2} \cdots p_k^{e_k}, \quad b = p_1^{f_1} p_2^{f_2} \cdots p_k^{f_k}
\]
the product \(ab\) is a perfect square if and only if \(e_i + f_i\) is even for all \(i\).

