   3 \times 5 = 15 \equiv 1 \pmod{7} \quad \Rightarrow \quad 3^{-1} \equiv 5 \pmod{7}.
   \]
   Now, multiply both sides of \( 3k \equiv 2 \) by \( 5 \):
   \[
   k \equiv 5 \times 2 \equiv 10 \equiv 3 \pmod{7}.
   \]
   Thus, we can express \( k \) as:
   \[
   k = 7m + 3 \quad \text{for some integer } m.
   \]

