\[
(a + b)^{12} = (|a + b| e^{i\theta})^{12} = |a + b|^{12} e^{12i\theta},
\]
where \(\theta\) is the argument of \(a + b\).

The magnitude \(|a + b|^{12}\) becomes:
\[
|a + b|^{12} = (2\cos\left(\frac{\pi (k - m)}{12}\right))^{12}.
\]

### Step 5: Determine Distinct Values
The distinct values of \((a + b)^{12}\) depend on the distinct values of \(\cos\left(\frac{\pi (k - m)}{12}\right)\). The values of \(k - m\) can range from \(-11\) to \(11\), which gives us the following distinct values for \(\cos\):
