   The vector \( \mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} \) is orthogonal to both \( \mathbf{b} \) and \( \mathbf{c} \). Since \( \mathbf{a} \) is also orthogonal to both \( \mathbf{b} \) and \( \mathbf{c} \), \( \mathbf{a} \) must be parallel to \( \mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} \).

3. **Calculate \( |\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c})| \)**:
   Since \( \mathbf{a} \) is a unit vector and is parallel to \( \mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} \), we have:
