   P(\text{at least one turns out}) = 1 - P(\text{none turn out}) = 1 - (1 - p)^n
   \]
   We want this probability to be at least \( \frac{3}{4} \):
   \[
   1 - (1 - p)^n \geq \frac{3}{4}
   \]

3. **Detailed calculations:**
   Rearranging the inequality gives:
   \[
   (1 - p)^n \leq \frac{1}{4}
   \]
   Substituting \( p = \frac{1}{5} \):
   \[
   \left( \frac{4}{5} \right)^n \leq \frac{1}{4}
   \]

   To solve for \( n \), we can take the logarithm of both sides. Using the natural logarithm:
   \[
   \ln\left( \left( \frac{4}{5} \right)^n \right) \leq \ln\left( \frac{1}{4} \right)
   \]
   This simplifies to:
